# config/Kconfig.linux — Linux compatibility
#
# The menu RFC-0053 section 8 lays out: unmodified Linux modules run in
# ring-3 servers (lxsrv-<class>), built from a pinned Linux tree
# (third_party/linux/) against a compatibility layer (lx/). None of that code
# exists beyond stage L0/L0b: the pinned submodule, the lx/ base skeleton,
# the module loader (SYS_MODULE_VERIFY / SYS_MODULE_MAP_X) and the empty
# server (LX_SERVER_SKELETON). So that no symbol here is silently inert,
# enabling any other fails the build: crates/core/limits/build.rs
# (`NOT_IMPLEMENTED`) refuses a .config that sets one, naming the stage that
# would implement it, and refuses LINUX_DRIVERS without LX_SERVER_SKELETON.
# When a stage lands, its symbols leave that list in the same change that
# wires them.
#
# Every symbol is default n: the default image contains no Linux-derived
# object.

menu "Linux compatibility"

config LINUX_DRIVERS
    bool "Linux driver support (ring-3 servers, RFC-0053)"
    default n
    depends on !PROFILE_EMBEDDED && !NO_MMU
    help
      Implemented up to RFC-0053 stage L0b: the module loader and the
      empty server. On its own it fails the build; it builds only with
      LX_SERVER_SKELETON (below), and every module option still fails.

      Runs validated, unmodified Linux modules in ring-3 server processes,
      one process per driver class, through a compatibility layer (lx/)
      over a pinned Linux tree (third_party/linux/). A server holds no
      actuator capability, and no safety decision reads its output.

      Licence: an image that contains a Linux module is distributed as
      GPL-2.0-only as a whole (Linux is GPL-2.0-only; the project is
      "Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only", and the combination takes the GPL
      branch). Shipping it requires offering the corresponding source.

      Needs an MMU and more memory than the Embedded profile has.

if LINUX_DRIVERS

config LX_SERVER_SKELETON
    bool "Server skeleton only (development, RFC-0053 L0)"
    default n
    help
      Builds the module loader's two syscalls (SYS_MODULE_VERIFY,
      SYS_MODULE_MAP_X, with the module digest table built into the
      kernel) and starts the empty Linux driver server LXSRV.ELF from its
      topology row (no capability). The server loads only the test
      module LXTEST.KO, compiled from this project's own C, and idles. No
      Linux object is built or linked: the image stays
      "Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only". Kernel feature `lx-server`.

config LX_LINUX_PIN
    string "Pinned Linux tag (stable tree)"
    default "v7.2.9"
    help
      The tag of third_party/linux/ (a shallow submodule of the
      kernel.org stable tree, fetched by tools/lx_fetch_linux.sh), fixed
      at RFC-0053 stage L0 and recorded with its commit in lx/LINUX_PIN.
      Every module is built against this exact tag; changing it is a
      "Lbump" (re-run every validated row). tools/lx_license_lint.py
      fails if this default, lx/LINUX_PIN and the submodule disagree.

menu "Filesystems (lxsrv-fs)"

config LX_FS_EXT2
    bool "ext2"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L1 (read-only, the pilot; needs
      owner sign-off), stage L2 (read-write). Enabling it fails the build.
      ext2 does no DMA: its server is isolated on every board.
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only image (see LINUX_DRIVERS).

config LX_FS_EXT4_RO
    bool "ext4 read-only (no journal replay)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L3 (ext4 + inert jbd2, mounted
      `noload`; a volume that needs recovery is refused). Enabling it
      fails the build. No DMA. Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_FS_EXT4_RW
    bool "ext4 read-write with journal (jbd2)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L4 (journalled writes, barrier
      mapping to BlockDevice::flush). Enabling it fails the build. No DMA.
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

endmenu

comment "Block devices: Linux filesystems use the native BlockDevice (RFC-0053 5.1); no Linux block driver is planned"

menu "USB (lxsrv-usb)"

config LX_USB_XHCI
    bool "xHCI host controller over PCI (xhci-pci, usbcore, hub)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L6 (QEMU qemu-xhci), after the
      native prerequisites of stage L5 (ring-3 DMA capability, PCI-BAR
      capability, ring-3 MSI, IOMMU wiring). Enabling it fails the build.
      Does DMA: see LX_ACCEPT_UNCONFINED_DMA. Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_USB_DWC3
    bool "Board USB controller (xhci-plat over dwc3)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L8 (VF2/K1 board bring-up of the
      dwc3 clocks, resets and PHY). Enabling it fails the build. Does
      DMA, and VF2/K1 have no IOMMU: needs LX_ACCEPT_UNCONFINED_DMA.
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_USB_HID
    bool "HID keyboard / mouse (usbhid)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L6. Enabling it fails the build.
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_USB_STORAGE
    bool "Mass storage (usb-storage)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L7 (host-template side only; the
      SCSI mid layer is not compiled). Enabling it fails the build.
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_USB_CDC
    bool "CDC serial (cdc-acm)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L7. Enabling it fails the build.
      Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_USB_UVC
    bool "UVC camera (uvcvideo)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L9+ (needs its own RFC amendment;
      V4L2 and videobuf2 are the heaviest class). Enabling it fails the
      build. Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

endmenu

menu "Later classes (each needs an RFC-0053 amendment)"

config LX_SND_ASOC
    bool "Audio (ALSA core + ASoC codecs)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L9+. Enabling it fails the build.
      Does DMA. Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_DRM_PANEL
    bool "Display panels (DRM/KMS panel drivers)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L9+ (panel drivers only, not GPU
      stacks). Enabling it fails the build. Does DMA. Licence:
      GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_IIO
    bool "IIO sensors"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L9+ (one driver per sensor, usually
      I2C/SPI, no DMA). No safety decision may read an IIO server's
      output (RFC-0053 9.4). Enabling it fails the build. Licence:
      GPL-2.0-only image.

config LX_WIFI
    bool "Wi-Fi (mac80211 / cfg80211)"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L9+, last, only with a product
      case. Enabling it fails the build. Does DMA. Licence: GPL-2.0-only
      image.

config LX_BLUETOOTH
    bool "Bluetooth"
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stage L9+. Enabling it fails the build.
      Does DMA. Licence: GPL-2.0-only image.

endmenu

config LX_ACCEPT_UNCONFINED_DMA
    bool "Allow DMA-capable Linux servers on a board without an IOMMU"
    default n
    depends on LX_USB_XHCI || LX_USB_DWC3 || LX_SND_ASOC || LX_DRM_PANEL || LX_WIFI || LX_BLUETOOTH
    help
      Not implemented: RFC-0053 stages L5/L8. Enabling it fails the build.

      Where a Linux server drives a bus master and the board has no
      IOMMU (VF2 and K1 have none), the device can write any physical
      address, including kernel memory, and the ring-3 boundary does not
      contain it (RFC-0053 9.2). A DMA class on such a board is meant to
      build only with this option set, so the acceptance is a recorded
      build-time act, never inherited silently; the server prints
      "[LX] DMA UNCONFINED" at start.

endif # LINUX_DRIVERS

config LX_MODULE_TOKENS
    int "Module verification tokens live at once"
    range 1 64
    default 4 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4 if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 4 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 4
    help
      Slots in the table of one-shot tokens SYS_MODULE_VERIFY grants and
      SYS_MODULE_MAP_X consumes (crates/core/syscall/src/module_ops.rs,
      built only with LX_SERVER_SKELETON). Each token is bound to the
      verifying task and is dropped when that task exits. A grant with
      every slot taken evicts one, and its owner must verify again.

      Cost: 24 B of .bss per slot on riscv64 and aarch64 (96 B at the
      default), and a scan of the table per verify, map and task exit
      (a few instructions per slot; nothing when LX_SERVER_SKELETON is off).
      Raise it only when more Linux driver servers than this start at once.
      Outside the LINUX_DRIVERS block so the constant always exists.

config USERSPACE_GPL
    bool "Allow third-party GPL userspace programs (e.g. BusyBox)"
    default n
    help
      The switch without which no third-party GPL program is built or put
      in an image. The kernel and the base image never contain one; with
      this on, a program below may be built from a pinned upstream release
      (fetched by URL and SHA-256, never committed) and copied onto a
      volume that is then distributed under that program's licence, with
      the source offer `make busybox-source-offer` writes (GPL-2.0
      section 3). Off by default.

config BUSYBOX
    bool "BusyBox (static, run through the Linux personality)"
    default n
    depends on USERSPACE_GPL && LINUX_ABI
    help
      `make busybox` builds the pinned BusyBox release (Makefile:
      BUSYBOX_VERSION, BUSYBOX_SHA256) from a tarball already on disk
      (BUSYBOX_TARBALL, outside the repository; make never downloads),
      checks its SHA-256, and builds a static binary with `zig cc`
      (BUSYBOX_ZIG or LX_ZIG; zig carries musl and the Linux uapi headers
      for both ISAs). It runs as BUSYBOX.ELF under its own topology row
      with abi = "linux". The make target refuses, naming what is missing,
      without zig or the tarball, or on a digest mismatch.
      GPL-2.0-only: see USERSPACE_GPL for the source offer.

config LINUX_ABI
    bool "Linux syscall personality (static Linux binaries, RFC-0047)"
    default n
    depends on !NO_MMU
    help
      Off by default in every profile (owner, 2026-10-04: running Linux
      programs is parked; Linux DRIVERS are LINUX_DRIVERS and do not need
      this). The QEMU test defconfigs turn it on so the gate keeps testing it.

      Run a static Linux binary (riscv64 or aarch64, asm-generic syscall
      table) unmodified: the kernel translates each Linux call onto the
      native one (crates/core/syscall/src/linux.rs). Only an image whose
      signed topology row says abi = "linux" is translated; a row says it
      or the image is native, so no native image ever reaches this table.
      A Linux row holds no hardware capability (the topology refuses one),
      its seccomp profile lists the native calls its translations reach,
      and every capability check of the native call stands.

      Off, the table is compiled out and a Linux row is refused at spawn.
      Answered today: file, pipe, directory, memory, time, identity and
      tty calls; clone in the fork and vfork shapes (the child is
      seeded from its row and shares the parent's open descriptions),
      wait4, and execve (into another Linux row only with a launch
      grant); threads (clone with CLONE_THREAD, futex, set_tid_address,
      exit and exit_group: a process's threads share its memory,
      capabilities and descriptors); signals inside the compartment (kill,
      tgkill, sigaction, sigprocmask, sigreturn, SIGCHLD, SIGPIPE, console
      ^C), mprotect (read/write exactly; adding PROT_EXEC is refused), and
      console input for the shell's foreground job. Not yet: posix_spawn's
      CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK child with a stack, requeue and
      priority-inheritance futexes.

endmenu # Linux compatibility
